"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 11.4 and the pathological findings of Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) with a clinically T2 stage. Favorable elements include negative surgical margins, no seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and the lack of documented nodal sampling, which widens uncertainty regarding nodal status. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison is limited as the Gleason pattern and ISUP report from the initial biopsy are missing.\n\nThe primary prognostic determinant noted is the CAPRA-S score of 3.0. Prognosis revision would be significantly aided by postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory, which are currently unknown. Furthermore, obtaining definitive nodal status information would refine risk stratification. The current data suggests a need for further follow-up to better characterize recurrence risk. The fixed estimated horizon is 69.39 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 45.11–93.68 months; its coverage has not been validated."
